Upstream fixes applied to the bundled iceberg-cpp; the nix package applies
the same patch (see nix/iceberg-cpp/default.nix).

- IcebergThirdpartyToolchain.cmake: reuse an existing avrocpp_s target (from
  Tenzir's avro plugin) instead of fetching and defining it again, and do
  not look up an already-found system avro-cpp package again: its config
  defines ALIAS targets unconditionally and fails the second time.
- Resolve utf8proc after Arrow: a static Arrow package finds utf8proc
  through its own find module, which defines utf8proc::utf8proc
  unconditionally and fails when the target exists already. utf8proc's
  package config skips existing targets.
- Fetch CRoaring's checksum-pinned amalgamation files instead
  of its repository archive containing benchmark data; disable CPM for
  custom CRoaring source archives. Keep system-package discovery intact.
- arrow_s3_file_io.cc: EnsureS3Initialized() fails (and caches the failure)
  when the host application already initialized Arrow's S3 subsystem, which
  Tenzir's s3 plugin always does; treat that as already-initialized.
- partition_summary.cc: PartitionSummary::Update casts every partition value
  to the partition field type, but LiteralCaster::CastTo refuses null
  literals, so any data file with a null partition value fails to commit.
  The stats themselves handle null fine (contains_null); feed it directly.
- url_encoder.cc: uses int8_t without including <cstdint>.
- error_handlers.cc, http_client.cc: a commit POST whose response never
  arrives (connection reset, timeout) surfaced as a generic REST error, so
  Transaction::Commit's Finalize deleted the manifest list it had written
  even though the server may have applied the commit, leaving the committed
  snapshot pointing at a deleted file and the table unreadable. Report the
  no-response case as CommitStateUnknown instead: Finalize then preserves
  the commit artifacts, and callers resolve the unknown outcome against the
  reloaded table (see the commit_uncertain test of the to_iceberg operator).
  Mutating requests (Post, Delete) additionally stop sharing the connection
  pool: when a reused keep-alive connection dies before the response,
  libcurl transparently re-sends the request, so the client sees the
  re-send's 409 instead of the lost response, treats the commit as a plain
  conflict, and the retry's re-Apply deletes manifests the server-side
  committed snapshot references. Fresh connections make a lost response
  surface as the transport error it is.

